      "writtenOn": "2025-07-08T00:29:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Just a note: I\u0027ve been thinking that for consistency we might also want to check `devtlb_allowed` here, in legacy mode, and set translation type to CONTEXT_TT_DEV_IOTLB instead of CONTEXT_TT_MULTI_LEVEL if device TLB is allowed.\n\nBut looks like the host IOMMU driver doesn\u0027t do that (since it doesn\u0027t set info-\u003eats_supported in this case), at least not for devices with ATC_REQUIRED, with the following comment:\n\n```\n\tsatcu \u003d dmar_find_matched_satc_unit(dev);\n\tif (satcu)\n\t\t/*\n\t\t * This device supports ATS as it is in SATC table.\n\t\t * When IOMMU is in legacy mode, enabling ATS is done\n\t\t * automatically by HW for the device that requires\n\t\t * ATS, hence OS should not enable this device ATS\n\t\t * to avoid duplicated TLB invalidation.\n\t\t */\n\t\treturn !(satcu-\u003eatc_required \u0026\u0026 !sm_supported(iommu));\n```\n\nSo, as I understand, we shouldn\u0027t do that in pKVM either. Or do that but only for devices without ATC_REQUIRED, but that means some extra logic / complexity to check for ATC_REQUIRED, so we\u0027d better not do that until we have a real use case for ATS in legacy mode.\n\nPlease let me know if this understanding is incorrect.\n\nAlso please let me know if this HW behavior (ATS working fully transparently in legacy mode, including device TLB invalidations) is documented in VT-d spec or elsewhere.",
      "revId": "7785a9a1b28aef92c97af045209ea09cdfce277c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
